---
import BaseLayout from '../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import ArticleSchema from '../../components/ArticleSchema.astro';
import FaqSection from '../../components/FaqSection.astro';
import { site } from '../../content';
const path = "/alternatives/smsbump-alternative";
const title = "SMSBump alternatives: accept the replacement after retirement";
const desc = "Choose a supported SMSBump replacement by proving destination capture, sending identity, event jobs and outstanding recovery work.";
const faqs = [
  {
    "question": "Can I keep SMSBump for modest campaigns?",
    "answer": "Its current lifecycle notice says the sending service is decommissioned. Choose a supported destination; resolve historical records and account questions separately."
  },
  {
    "question": "Must I remove Yotpo loyalty or reviews?",
    "answer": "Evaluate those dependencies separately. Preserve useful event inputs and demonstrate that the replacement receives the required customer identifiers and properties."
  },
  {
    "question": "Does a migrated contact count prove readiness?",
    "answer": "No. Verify channel permission, capture routes, active sending identity, queues, purchase exits, reply handling and costs. Mark unavailable historical evidence explicitly."
  }
];
---
<BaseLayout title={title} description={desc} site={site} path={path}>
  <ArticleSchema title={title} description={desc} siteName={site.name} domain={site.domain} path={path} />
  <article class="container py-16 prose-shopify">
    <a href="/alternatives">All alternatives</a>
    <h1>{title}</h1>
    <p>SMSBump is a historical sending identity, not a current starter option. The replacement decision now concerns a supported service and the work it must own, including gaps left by an incomplete move. Preserve useful loyalty and customer data relationships without treating a retired messaging backend as active.</p>
    <h2>Start with the lifecycle notice</h2>
    <p>The current <a href="https://smsbump.com/get-started">SMSBump notice</a>, checked November 25, 2026, says Yotpo SMS is decommissioned and its dashboard and background services are inactive. Yotpo’s <a href="https://www.yotpo.com/blog/email-sms-migration-partners/">migration announcement</a> scheduled full messaging deprecation for December 31, 2025. A new procurement brief should therefore specify a supported replacement.</p><p>The <a href="/apps/smsbump">legacy app review</a> covers recovery of held records. This page instead accepts the destination’s daily operation. Do not plan a live side-by-side trial against retired sending, or promise that the old dashboard will export missing evidence. Keep any existing supported replacement working while you investigate exceptions.</p>
    <h2>Choose against the jobs still needed</h2>
    <p>Inventory launch texts, welcome flows, cart reminders, subscriber exclusions, loyalty events and reply handling. For every job record the producing app, customer key, event properties, destination sender and person who fixes failures. Loyalty points and review data can remain valuable inputs even though Yotpo messaging retired; retaining those products is a separate decision from replacing delivery.</p><p>Ask <a href="/apps/postscript">Postscript</a> and <a href="/apps/attentive">Attentive</a> to demonstrate a required text job, and <a href="/apps/klaviyo">Klaviyo</a> or <a href="/apps/omnisend">Omnisend</a> to demonstrate combined-channel ownership where that suits the team. These are candidates, not ranked winners. Require proof of the current integration, fields and destination availability in the proposed account. A <a href="/apps/sequenzy">Sequenzy evaluation</a> must establish its own event and channel path rather than inherit an old email-only assumption.</p>
    <h2>Accept current capture rather than merely imported totals</h2>
    <p>Trace every old keyword, QR code, packaging instruction, popup and checkout capture into the new service. Keep the original source and disclosure alongside each channel’s status. Reconcile imported subscribed, withdrawn and unresolved records individually; equal total counts cannot prove permission continuity. Preserve email status separately from SMS status.</p><p>Submit controlled signups on the actual replacement forms. Confirm customer matching, incentive delivery and the source evidence saved. Then withdraw permission and inspect both the campaign audience and delayed flow queue. Retire broken old capture routes or publish working instructions. Use <a href="/guides/list-growth">capture planning</a> and <a href="/guides/consent">permission evidence</a> to define acceptance. Missing historical evidence remains unresolved even if a new form works.</p>
    <h2>Resolve the sender and customer-service endpoint</h2>
    <p>Require written provider confirmation of the active number, transfer outcome or replacement identity, registration status and reply endpoint. A number in an archive is not evidence that the destination can use it. Exercise STOP and HELP on the active path with controlled records and verify who sees other customer replies.</p><p>Separate promotional sending from order updates and unfinished service work. Record open replies, missing-order questions and escalation responsibility before retiring an inbox or connector. Service communication must follow its own purpose and permission requirements; labelling a discount as service does not establish eligibility. Assign a receiving team and a fallback contact route if old reply history cannot be recovered.</p>
    <h2>Rebuild events without resurrecting old queues</h2>
    <p>Use a fresh controlled order and record its identifier through the producer and destination. Exercise duplicate delivery, delayed purchase, cancellation, refund and a customer whose phone changed. A historical import must not start a new welcome or cart sequence. Mark old scheduled jobs as unavailable, cancelled or already recreated using records held; do not claim an inactive backend is still processing them.</p><table class="data-table"><thead><tr><th>Fixture</th><th>Acceptance question</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>4,200 requests for 80 pieces</td><td>Who allocates stock and cancels sold-out invitations?</td></tr><tr><td>VIP early link without codes</td><td>Which eligibility snapshot gates access and prevents a duplicate general send?</td></tr><tr><td>City-specific tour or seasonal drop</td><td>Who checks local timing, current variants and expiry?</td></tr><tr><td>Gift buyer or active subscriber</td><td>Does the intended education or exclusion survive the event mapping?</td></tr></tbody></table><p>These are proposed fixtures, not observed outcomes. Test one launch message and a separately justified stock reminder; do not infer that another text improves results. Preserve style preferences and value segmentation without inventing conversion uplifts. See <a href="/guides/segment-by-value">segment planning</a>.</p>
    <h2>Price the replacement and close the old account separately</h2>
    <p>Obtain a current quote for a quiet month and a peak launch month: platform minimum, profiles, regional text rates, encoded segments, carrier fees, registration, optional service, integration maintenance and support labor. Include unresolved old invoices separately so they do not disappear inside a new vendor’s attribution report. Confirm transfer charges and contractual closure with the relevant provider; retirement does not prove every billing dispute is settled.</p><p>For illustration, a 100 gross order less a 10 incentive and 45 variable costs leaves 45 before message and support costs. If net revenue is already 90, 90 minus 45 is the same 45; do not subtract the incentive twice. Compare contribution against a suitable holdout using consistent refunds and reporting windows, rather than comparing incompatible attributed revenue totals. <a href="/guides/pricing">Pricing economics</a> and <a href="/guides/offer-discipline">offer discipline</a> support the worksheet.</p>
    <h2>Release a supported operating plan</h2>
    <p>Build the next quarter’s calendar around actual launch, replenishment and shipping needs. Give each job one sender, one queue owner and explicit customer exclusions. Keep education, gift offers and scarcity claims tied to the customer’s purpose and stock truth. Record recipient-time scheduling, combined-channel caps and cancellation timing using <a href="/guides/quiet-hours">quiet-hours planning</a> and <a href="/guides/campaign-restraint">restraint</a>.</p><p>Accept when current capture reaches the supported service, permission withdrawals exclude recipients, events create only intended jobs, purchases cancel the relevant reminders, replies reach staff and cost ownership is clear. Save evidence and unresolved records with a named owner. If a new release fails, pause that path and reconcile sends already made; rollback means a known supported configuration, not reactivation of SMSBump. No calendar deadline or revenue level substitutes for these checks.</p>
    <FaqSection faqs={faqs} />
  </article>
</BaseLayout>
